WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.590
2022 marks 50 years since formal relations

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.285
began between Australia and China.

00:00:10.285 --> 00:00:13.953
But it's an anniversary neither
side seem to be celebrating.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:17.220
We start with the significant

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.340
worsening of relations
between China and Australia.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.615
Beijing is raging a politically charged

00:00:22.615 --> 00:00:24.810
trade war Down Under.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.380
One of China's
government-sponsored scholars

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.180
describe Australian
foreign policy as being

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.945
bizarre, immature, belligerent,
juvenile and mindless.

00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:37.260
Every time now there is a headline

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.880
with Australia and China relations

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.846
we almost know immediately
it's going to be bad news.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.220
A series of disputes
between the two nations

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.800
has led China to introduce
crippling trade bans

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.351
on some of Australia's biggest exports.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.450
Almost a dozen Australian commodities

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.790
are still subject to
official, and in many cases

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.130
unofficial, sanctions by
the Chinese government,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.600
depriving Australian exporters

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.260
access to billions of
dollars worth of a market.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.280
Because Australia is so closely

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.200
tied to China economically,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.236
it means that China has a lot of trade

00:01:09.236 --> 00:01:11.520
leverage over Australia

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.810
that it can use

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.380
if Australia does things
that it doesn't approve of.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.730
You can use the word sanction

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.220
or you can use the word
trade friction for that.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.380
But both of those things
refer to the same point,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.820
a process of coercion,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.460
to try and change Australia's
political settings.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.560
This isn't any old diplomatic row.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.440
It's about how the West
handles the Chinese government

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.240
that not only wants to be a major player

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.590
in this political game,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.080
but wants to decide the rules as well.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.810
Australia is really the
canary in the coal mine.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.450
What happens in Australia
in relation to China

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.290
over the next decade will be a blueprint

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.850
for how the rest of the world

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.623
will handle China in coming years.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.747
Much of China's economic
miracle since the 1980s

00:02:06.747 --> 00:02:09.093
has been driven by industrialization,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:14.010
building roads, buildings,
machinery and factories,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.290
all of which require steel.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.533
And to make steel, you
need two ingredients:

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.473
iron ore for the iron content

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.470
and metallurgical coal to provide

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.570
the energy in the blast furnace.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.272
Australia is the world's preeminent

00:02:26.272 --> 00:02:29.730
export supplier of both
of those commodities.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.110
This was initially the basis

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.870
for the relationship
starting in the 1980s,

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.050
but really gathered steam
during the mid 2000s

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.353
as China's heaviest stage of
industrialization took off.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.410
Australia began to experience

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.810
what was known as the mining boom,

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.710
and the mining boom

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.260
was a time of enormous
wealth in Australia.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.010
So billions and billions and
billions of dollars of income

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.660
flowed into the country,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.240
and Australia became enormously
rich and comfortable.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.636
And that mining boom was
thanks entirely to China.

00:03:00.636 --> 00:03:03.090
Mineral resources became

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.000
the foundation of the relationship.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.500
But as China's wealth grew,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.380
so did its demand for luxury goods.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.390
Wine and rock lobster became popular

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.090
among China's burgeoning
middle and upper classes,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.100
which provided Australian exporters

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.880
even more opportunities
and cemented trade ties.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.470
Two-way trade grew from

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.820
US$86 million

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.463
in the early years of
our diplomatic relations,

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:34.590
to US$136.4 billion in 2013,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.510
and China has been Australia's

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.543
biggest trading partner
for five consecutive years.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.330
Australia also developed

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.520
a number of other export industries,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.060
which China was in need of as
its development level rose.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.670
Probably the most famous
is international education,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.350
where tens, and in some cases,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.240
hundreds of thousands of Chinese students

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.063
came to Australian
universities to study degrees.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.150
In 2019, the income raised

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.090
from Chinese students studying
in Australia was valued

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.929
at around A$12 billion.

00:04:08.929 --> 00:04:10.350
That same year,

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.250
1.4 million Chinese tourists
spent over $12 billion,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.950
contributing to more than
one quarter of Australia's

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.263
entire international tourism spend.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.650
But while trade relations have thrived,

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.100
politically the two nations
have kept their distance.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.720
For most of the last 30 years,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.910
the way that that circle has been squared

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.880
is attempt to keep the two
things in separate discussions.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.950
We'll have a discussion
about the political,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.400
where we'll be able to disagree
and have disputes at times,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.590
whereas on the economic,
we can have a discussion

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.880
solely about the economic,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.220
and where those mutual benefits lie.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.350
The challenge we've had
over the last five years

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.020
is that kind of firewall
between the two sides

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.300
has become harder and harder to sustain,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.250
as many of those political difficulties

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.950
have become stronger and stronger.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.583
Is he on the side of the
agencies that keep us safe,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.705
or is he on the side of
a foreign government?

00:05:03.705 --> 00:05:06.900
In 2017, Australian Labor Senator

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.110
Sam Dastyari resigned from
parliament after allegations

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:12.540
that he'd received money
from Chinese donors

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.153
affiliated with the
Chinese Communist Party.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.320
Shortly after, former Prime
Minister Malcolm Turnbull

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.270
passed foreign interference laws

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.490
that targeted cases of espionage

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:24.870
and required people acting

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.820
in the interest of overseas powers

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.103
to register and disclose their ties.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.000
Malcolm Turnbull at the
time, while professing

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.490
that the legislation was
not targeted at China,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.310
said that the Australian
people have now stood up

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.020
in his introduction for it,

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.290
which echoed what Mao Zedong said

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:46.980
when he started the
Chinese People's Republic.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.170
Modern China was founded in 1949

00:05:51.051 --> 00:05:53.450
with these words...

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.260
The Chinese people have stood up.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.840
That was considered very insulting,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.970
and pretty much laid out exactly

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.913
who this legislation
was designed to target.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.390
A lot of Chinese Australians
in the community,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:13.350
who had connections with China,
whether it be through trade,

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.080
whether it would be personal
network connections,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.850
they felt they were targets of that

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.100
foreign interference law.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.720
10 years ago or more,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.150
those connections were really prized.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.710
And these people,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.500
those who had connections
with the mainland,

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.320
whether through business,
personal or political,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.770
were really valuable for
Australia-China relations.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.510
You know, at the end of 2016,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.970
people with those kind of
connections were really

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.603
seen as vectors of Chinese
Communist Party influence.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.380
The Foreign Interference Act

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.880
came amid mounting concern

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.254
about China's influence
over Australian politics.

00:06:51.254 --> 00:06:53.970
What followed were a number
of political disputes

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.733
that eroded bilateral
relations even further.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.420
China's ambassador on the attack,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.690
renewing criticism of
Australia's ban on Huawei,

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.400
preventing the Chinese telco

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.480
from participating in
Australia's 5G rollout.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.170
As I see it, it's basically
political motivated.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.850
China says Australia's decision

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.550
to offer safe haven to Hong Kong residents

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.700
is a serious violation
of international law.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.000
Today, the PM hit back at
China's escalating rhetoric

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.100
after Beijing said Hong Kong protestors

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.170
were showing signs of terrorism.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:29.880
China has rejected suggestions

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.360
it's behind a large-scale
cyber attack on Australia.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.920
No one was ever arguing,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.770
that there shouldn't be an
independent investigation

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:41.070
into the origins of the
virus. But it's curious,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.149
why did Canberra have to
take the lead on this?

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.910
There's been a number of key points

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.250
along the bilateral timeline where we can

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:54.300
sort of pinpoint that spiraling
downward of relationship.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.460
And then when Australia called

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.320
for international investigation

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.900
into the origins of the coronavirus,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.810
that really put China on attention.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.440
We will need an independent inquiry

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.420
that looks at what has occurred here

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.430
so we can learn the lessons.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.050
Many, I guess on the China side,

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:16.770
felt like issues which
are difficult to navigate

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.470
shouldn't be broadcasted by megaphone.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.623
They should be addressed
quietly behind doors.

00:08:34.807 --> 00:08:36.810
Now, that inquiry was ultimately supported

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:39.480
and conducted by the World
Health Assembly in that year.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.270
But the Chinese government
was extremely upset

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:45.060
about Australia's going
forward and going first

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.980
in terms of that international call.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.380
And the result was a
series of trade sanctions

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:52.200
applied to nearly a dozen
Australian commodities

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.711
over the latter part of 2020.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.530
In May 2020, an 80% tariff

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.300
was put on imports of barley,

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.640
and restrictions were
placed on imports of beef,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.800
which had brought in nearly $3 billion

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.348
for Australian farmers the year before.

00:09:07.348 --> 00:09:09.240
China announced an investigation

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.800
into Australian wine imports,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.142
claiming that Australia was dumping

00:09:13.142 --> 00:09:14.640
excess wine on the market.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.741
Eventually that led to
tariffs in excess of 200%

00:09:17.741 --> 00:09:19.530
being put on Australian wine,

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.097
effectively killing the
trade almost entirely.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.260
Some of these Chinese sanctions

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.940
have been put on in official manner,

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.610
particularly for barley and wine and coal.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.500
But in some other agricultural products,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.785
they've used gray-zone tactics.

00:09:33.785 --> 00:09:35.880
So these aren't official bans,

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.530
but unofficially these products

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.690
don't get passed through Chinese customs,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.700
and therefore can't be
sold in the country.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:43.560
And it's very hard to be able to get

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.530
traction on them using
international trade law

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.330
because you would first have to prove

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:49.622
that it's even occurring.

00:09:51.668 --> 00:09:53.640
Before these recent tariffs

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.110
were placed on Australia,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:57.990
China purchased around
a third of all exports,

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:01.950
worth more than A$150 billion.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.690
Now, with restrictions in place,

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.780
some industries have
fared better than others.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.619
Coal provides an illustrative example,

00:10:08.619 --> 00:10:10.410
as the effect of that sanction

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.420
was just a musical-chairs arrangement.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.610
Different people were
selling to different people,

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.350
but not really any overall loss

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.210
for any players in the market.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.790
An alternative example would
be for some of the exporters

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:24.930
who don't have alternate viable
markets available to them.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.240
In most years, China would buy

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.933
over 90% of Australia's
rock lobster exports.

00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:32.640
And having that market
shut down overnight,

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.700
there was not really any
other deep markets in Asia,

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.280
where you could place
that many rock lobsters.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.930
Wine suffered a similar fate

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.850
as did Australian timber products.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.535
So the effects ranged
from mild to negligible,

00:10:44.535 --> 00:10:47.880
to very catastrophic for other
small industries as well.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.880
While China is economically

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.375
critical for Australia,
the reverse is less true.

00:10:54.375 --> 00:10:57.960
Some 70% of Australia's
exports are resources

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.783
for which there are alternative suppliers.

00:11:00.783 --> 00:11:03.750
However, there's one Australian export,

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.230
which can equate to up to
10% of the country's GDP,

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.153
and it remains critical to China.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.341
China keeps buying Australian iron ore.

00:11:13.341 --> 00:11:15.630
Any sign that could change?

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.080
Well, no, quite frankly, China needs it.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.929
China is the world's
largest buyer of iron ore,

00:11:21.929 --> 00:11:24.473
not just in Asia, but in the world.

00:11:24.473 --> 00:11:26.594
And Australia for its part

00:11:26.594 --> 00:11:29.136
is the world's largest
exporter of iron ore,

00:11:29.136 --> 00:11:31.470
about 80% of which goes to China.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.540
So when you have the world's largest buyer

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.630
and the world's largest
seller in the same region,

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:40.800
for both sides, there's not
really any other alternative.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.240
At the height of the trade dispute

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.980
between Australia and China,

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.470
there were all these questions
about, why doesn't China

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.300
just get its iron ore from somewhere else?

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.530
And certainly, they've tried.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.050
They've tried to get iron ore from Brazil.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.120
They've tried to get iron ore from Africa.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.580
But the deposits in Africa
aren't developed enough,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.430
and the ones in Brazil
are famously unreliable.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:04.230
China couldn't cut off the
iron-ore trade with Australia.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.500
And because of that, China's
coercive trade measures

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:11.070
could not be as effective
as they would otherwise be.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:14.940
For all the diplomatic bluster
and the trade coercion,

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.460
Australia and China
were still held together

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.470
deep beneath all the noise and the fury

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.972
by this tight link of iron ore.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:28.920
While the iron-ore sector

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.050
kept some form of trade ties intact,

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:34.181
the diplomatic side of the
relationship broke down.

00:12:34.181 --> 00:12:36.300
For two years, there were no high level

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.100
discussions between the countries.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.610
Australia said it tried to reach out

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.620
to its ministerial counterparts in China,

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:45.840
and it just wouldn't get a response,

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.760
which at some levels is frustrating,

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.894
for instance on trade
relations, on foreign relations.

00:12:50.894 --> 00:12:51.870
And in some cases it's dangerous,

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.600
when it comes to things
like defense and military,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.070
especially as Australia was still

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.650
running operations in the South China Sea,

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.770
and in waters not too far
away from China itself.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:03.930
So effectively for two
years, Australia and China

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:07.440
didn't have diplomatic
relations at a high level.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:08.670
And that coincided with

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.340
the rise of Wolf Warrior
diplomacy in China,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.380
which meant that instead of talking,

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:14.550
there was a lot of yelling

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.710
from Beijing directed at Australia.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.270
On the other side, however,

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.040
you then had the Morrison government,

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.327
who were very much hyping
up the threat from China.

00:13:23.327 --> 00:13:24.930
They were talking a lot about

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.820
the drums of war beating in Asia,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.500
and comparing the current times

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:32.370
to the late 1930s before World War II.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.755
That kind of strident rhetoric

00:13:34.755 --> 00:13:37.320
under the previous government
really didn't sit well

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.840
with their Chinese
counterparts in Beijing.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.470
It didn't give them any sense
of space into navigating

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:46.914
those delicate and complex
international issues.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.500
But again, I think, you
know, China has some

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:04.530
blame to bear in the state
of bilateral relations.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.094
Its issuing of those 14 grievances

00:14:07.094 --> 00:14:09.603
really puts Australia in a difficult spot.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.540
One piece of paper, 14 points,

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.910
a diplomatic shot from
Beijing at Canberra,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.923
that's been heard around the world.

00:14:16.923 --> 00:14:19.050
In November 2020,

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.240
a list was leaked to the Australian media,

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.250
allegedly from the Chinese government,

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:26.323
containing 14 grievances that
would need to be addressed

00:14:26.323 --> 00:14:29.204
before a trading
relationship could resume.

00:14:29.204 --> 00:14:32.130
The list included restricting free media

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:33.780
and preventing parliamentarians

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:36.042
from speaking out against China.

00:14:36.042 --> 00:14:38.340
The problem with the 14 grievances is that

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:42.420
they put Australia and China
in a very difficult position.

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:45.450
Because Australia couldn't do any of them,

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:46.560
because if it did them,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.770
it would be seen as giving
up a lot of its democracy

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:53.070
and its free speech to appease China.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.480
And China couldn't give up on them,

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.390
because they presented them to
Australia in the first place.

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.714
It ends up being a bit of a hole,

00:14:58.714 --> 00:15:00.990
which neither side could
dig themselves out of.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:04.440
The Chinese ambassador to Australia

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.710
has since said the 14
points were misinterpreted.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.797
Eventually, the concerns were being

00:15:09.797 --> 00:15:11.390
reported in a twisted way

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:15.480
as the so-called
preconditions, as demands.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.100
This is not true,

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.040
that's not true.

00:15:21.343 --> 00:15:23.340
Heightened political rhetoric

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.846
was amplified on both sides.

00:15:25.846 --> 00:15:29.370
The result was a polarization
of public opinion

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.443
and communities caught in the crossfire.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.010
The consequence of that not being specific

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.228
and not being targeted in talking about,

00:15:38.228 --> 00:15:40.710
you know, what aspects
of China are we talking?

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:41.790
Are we talking about the government?

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:43.770
Are we talking about the Communist Party?

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:45.270
Chinese Australians got caught

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:49.380
in that middle of that
change and that reassessment.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:53.130
What happens in that
sort of looking top down

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:57.630
is you miss the granular
and the more complex issues,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:01.230
that arise from community
and social side of things.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:06.230
And that is discrimination
and racism being perpetrated

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.213
because of the state
of bilateral relations.

00:16:10.082 --> 00:16:11.700
And I think the first and second year

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:13.560
of the Being Chinese in Australia survey

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.900
really showed one of that consequences.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.930
And that was high rates of discrimination

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:22.353
and threats to Chinese
Australians' physical safety.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.420
Since Anthony Albanese's Labor Party

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.300
won a national election in May 2022,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.340
leaders from both countries have signaled

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.679
that they want to ease the
tensions of recent years.

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:38.970
Since Albanese actually came to power,

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.880
we've seen a far more subtle shift

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:44.160
in how his government is handling

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:45.900
the relationship with China.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:47.880
They seem to be doing two things at once.

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:50.580
So on one hand, they are
maintaining that line.

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.040
They are speaking up on human rights.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.390
On the other hand,

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.300
they are very carefully exploring

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.630
how they can improve relations
with the Chinese government.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.900
We've seen the first
high-level diplomatic contact

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.207
between Australia and China in years.

00:17:06.207 --> 00:17:08.436
But mending relations

00:17:08.436 --> 00:17:10.807
has not been without its challenges.

00:17:10.807 --> 00:17:12.750
A new security deal signed

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:16.350
between the Solomon Islands
and China in April 2022

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:18.270
has officials worried that it could pave

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.224
the way for a Chinese
naval base in the Pacific.

00:17:21.224 --> 00:17:24.873
It also raised concerns
beyond just the APAC region.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.960
It was a shockwave to
the US and Australia,

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:32.190
and it meant that they then
rushed back out to the Pacific

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:34.050
to try and repair relations

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:35.460
that they, I guess, hadn't really known

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.320
were broken in the first place.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:38.520
And so now the Pacific has become

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.838
an incredibly important part of the global

00:17:40.838 --> 00:17:43.743
strategic contest
between the US and China.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.700
The regional dynamics have shifted.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:51.330
China has indicated
its strategic ambitions

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:52.530
in the Indo-Pacific.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:54.930
And that really complicates

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:59.310
a lot of things for the
Australia-China relations.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:03.420
I would suspect that you can
have cordial trade relations

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:06.900
but disagree on a lot of
other international issues,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.410
whether it be security
in the Indo-Pacific.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.170
But I guess it all
comes back to, you know,

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:14.940
where can the two countries find

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.850
common points of agreement
and work on those?

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:19.835
And can the two countries

00:18:19.835 --> 00:18:23.670
agree to disagree on various issues?

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.400
What we've seen recently
is the greatest power shift

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.510
in modern history across the
Pacific, from the US to China.

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:32.160
We no longer have the comfort of the world

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:33.600
that's dominated by the US,

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:37.334
with whose values and political
system we largely share.

00:18:37.334 --> 00:18:39.450
Australia's often described as being

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:43.290
on the front lines of
the international debate

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.110
over how to handle the rise of China.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.210
The question is whether or not Australia

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:51.060
can have a trade relationship
with China again,

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:52.620
but a strategic relationship

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:54.900
that's far more focused on the US.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.990
Can Australia want different
things for the region

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.120
that maybe go against what China wants

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.570
and still live peacefully
and co-exist with China?

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:05.850
These are the questions
that are going to be asked

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:06.750
for years to come.

